1. What is Encapsulation?
Answer

Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (variables) and methods (functions) into a single 
unit called a class. It also restricts direct access to data by making variables private and providing 
controlled access through public methods.


2. What is Data Hiding?
Answer

Data Hiding is the process of preventing direct access to variables by declaring them as private. Data 
can only be accessed through public getter and setter methods.

3. Difference between Encapsulation and Data Hiding?
Encapsulation	Data Hiding
Wrapping variables and methods into one class.	Restricting direct access to data.
Achieved using classes.	Achieved using the private keyword.
Supports security and maintainability.	Protects data from unauthorized access.

Interview Line:

Data Hiding is one feature of Encapsulation.

4. Why do we make variables private?
Answer

To:

Protect data
Prevent unauthorized modification
Allow controlled access
Perform validation
Improve security
5. What are Getter and Setter methods?
Getter

Returns the value of a private variable.

Example

public String getName() {
    return name;
}
Setter

Assigns or updates the value of a private variable.

Example

public void setName(String name) {
    this.name = name;
}
6. Why do we use Getters and Setters?
Answer

They provide controlled access to private variables.

Advantages:

Validation
Security
Maintainability
Flexibility
7. What is a Fully Encapsulated Class?
Answer

A class is fully encapsulated when:

All instance variables are private.
Data is accessed through public methods.
Validation or business rules are applied before modifying data.

Example:

private double salary;

public void setSalary(double salary) {
    if(salary > 0)
        this.salary = salary;
}
8. Why is validation added inside setters?
Answer

To prevent invalid data from being stored.

Example

salary = -5000

should not be accepted.

Instead

if(salary > 0)

stores only valid values.

9. Can we access private variables directly?
Answer

No.

Wrong

obj.salary = 50000;

Correct

obj.setSalary(50000);
10. Why does System.out.println(object) call toString()?
Answer

Whenever an object is passed to println(), Java automatically calls

object.toString();

If we override toString(), meaningful information is printed.

11. Why do we override toString()?
Answer

Default output

Employee@2f92e0f4

After overriding

Employee[name=Bhaskar, salary=50000]

It improves readability.

12. What is @Override?
Answer

@Override is an annotation that tells the compiler we are overriding a parent class method.

It helps catch spelling mistakes during compilation.

13. Is @Override compulsory?
Answer

No.

But it is highly recommended because it prevents accidental errors.

14. Why does Java automatically call toString()?
Answer

Because

System.out.println(object);

internally becomes

System.out.println(object.toString());
15. Why does e1 and e2 use the same toString()?
Answer

Because methods belong to the class.

Objects only store data.

The same method executes for different objects using different object data.

16. What is this keyword?
Answer

this refers to the current object.

Example

this.salary = salary;

Left side

this.salary

instance variable

Right side

salary

local variable

17. Why do we write this.salary = salary?
Answer

Because both variable names are the same.

this.salary

refers to the instance variable.

salary

refers to the parameter.

18. What happens if we remove @Override?
Answer

Program still works.

But compiler won't check whether we are actually overriding the parent method.

19. What happens if we remove super()?
Answer

If the parent class has a no-argument constructor,

Java automatically inserts

super();
20. Why should constructors call setters?
Answer

Suppose setter validates salary.

If constructor directly assigns

this.salary = salary;

validation is skipped.

Better

setSalary(salary);

Now constructor also validates data.

